    <section id="case-konars">
      <div class="case-head"><span class="idx">Case 01</span></div>
      <h2>Building an ERP from scratch, with AI as the accelerant</h2>
      <div class="case-sub">Konars Group — Architecture &amp; Construction · Aug 2025 – Present</div>

      <div class="metrics-strip">
        <div><div class="num">1 month</div><div class="cap">from research to shipped product</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">1 engineer</div><div class="cap">on the build</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">50%</div><div class="cap">less time spent on research &amp; PRD drafting</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">~IDR 1B/mo</div><div class="cap">now processed through the system</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Problem</h3>
        <p>Konars ran its entire transaction process manually in Excel — no shared system, no real-time visibility, reconciliation that depended on people cross-checking spreadsheets by hand. Off-the-shelf ERP tools existed, but they were the wrong fit on two fronts at once: too complex for a team with no prior digital workflow, and too expensive to justify at this scale. Buying a tool meant forcing the team to adapt to the software. I chose to build a system that adapted to the team instead.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Approach</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Compressed research with AI.</strong> I used Claude to synthesize interviews and workflow observations with finance and ops staff — mapping exactly how they used Excel day to day, where errors crept in, and where reconciliation broke down. This cut research and PRD drafting time by roughly half.</li>
          <li><strong>Validated before building anything.</strong> A self-hosted working prototype went in front of the team within the first two weeks — before development started in earnest, so the build was shaped by real reactions, not assumptions.</li>
          <li><strong>Scoped ruthlessly.</strong> With one engineer and a month on the clock, I prioritized the core transaction workflow end-to-end over breadth — deliberately leaving advanced reporting and integrations for a later phase.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="shot">
        <strong>Add screenshot: before / after</strong>
        Old Excel-based tracking sheet next to the shipped ERP interface — the contrast is the whole story here.
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Outcome</h3>
        <p>Shipped in one month with a single engineer. The system now processes roughly IDR 1 billion a month in transactions that were previously tracked entirely by hand — replacing manual reconciliation with a system built around how the team already worked, not how a generic ERP assumed they should.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="case-efishery">
      <div class="case-head"><span class="idx">Case 02</span></div>
      <h2>Replacing gut-feel logistics with a data-driven system</h2>
      <div class="case-sub">eFishery — Group Leader of Product Manager · Oct 2023 – Feb 2025</div>

      <div class="metrics-strip">
        <div><div class="num">300M+ IDR</div><div class="cap">cut from logistics costs</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">350M+ IDR/mo</div><div class="cap">reduction in stock losses</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">&lt;2%</div><div class="cap">fulfillment error rate</div></div>
        <div><div class="num">+3%</div><div class="cap">margin improvement</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Problem</h3>
        <p>eFishery's platform coordinated operations, warehousing, and logistics across a large B2C network — but routing and inventory decisions were made without shared, real-time data. Teams were operating reactively: fulfillment errors and stock problems surfaced after they'd already cost money, because no one had visibility into the full picture until it was too late.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Approach</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Built shared visibility first.</strong> I defined platform-level KPIs and data instrumentation standards across teams — real-time fulfillment error rates, stock-out frequency by warehouse, routing cost per delivery — giving every team a common source of truth for the first time.</li>
          <li><strong>Modeled demand instead of reacting to it.</strong> Using historical sales and regional demand patterns, I built forecasting models that let routing and inventory planning happen ahead of problems rather than in response to them.</li>
          <li><strong>Redesigned sourcing with Pricing &amp; Ops.</strong> The same data fed into a reworked sourcing model — changing supplier selection and order timing to directly improve margins.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="shot">
        <strong>Add screenshot: KPI dashboard</strong>
        A simplified view of the instrumentation/KPI framework you introduced — makes the "no visibility before" claim visible.
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <h3>Outcome</h3>
        <p>Logistics costs down by 300M+ IDR, stock losses down 350M+ IDR a month, fulfillment errors held under 2%, and margins up 3% through the sourcing redesign — all built on a KPI framework that didn't exist before this work.</p>
      </div>
